WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
When the AI industry talks about safety,
they almost exclusively

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.640
mean behavioral alignment.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.240
Techniques like RLHF, RLAIF, and
constitutional AI are

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.360
built on the assumption that a
probabilistic system can be

5
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.840
trained to be intrinsically safe.

6
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.600
These techniques attempt to force a model
to reliably refuse

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.000
unsafe behaviors. They do this by
mathematically shaping its loss

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.800
surface during training, effectively
building a statistical barrier against generating

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.120
harmful outputs. The mathematical flaw in
this approach is that

10
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.200
the space of possible user inputs is
functionally infinite.

11
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.720
The gradient used to shape that loss
surface is simply

12
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.960
too imprecise to guarantee refusal across
all edge cases.

13
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.560
Once a model is deployed, the surface area
it faces

14
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.600
will always exceed its original training
distribution.

15
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.960
It encounters inputs and environments that
the loss surface was

16
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.640
never designed to handle.

17
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.520
Because failure modes naturally exist
where the weights fail to

18
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.160
catch them, treating a probabilistic
system as definitively safe from

19
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.760
inside its own weights is structurally
impossible.

20
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.000
This theoretical brittleness is backed by
a five-year empirical track

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.760
record. Alignment interventions routinely
fail under modest adversarial pressure.

22
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.760
This chart visualizes the failure rates of
25 different frontier

23
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.880
models against simple adversarial tactics.

24
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.520
When researchers prompt these models using
adversarial poetry, literally just

25
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.440
writing a harmful request in verse, the
model's refusal mechanism

26
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.240
is bypassed 62% of the time.

27
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.960
If you wrap that same harmful content in a
basic

28
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.640
JSON structure, a tactic known as a format
lock attack,

29
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.400
you bypass current frontier systems 30 to
42% of the

30
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.920
time. Place those models in a multi-agent
context, and the

31
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.320
safety alignment breaks at a rate of 40%,
with a

32
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.200
median time to failure of just 16 minutes.

33
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.360
Bypasses like these emerge directly from
the nature of generative

34
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.360
probability rather than isolated,
patchable software bugs.

35
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.400
Betting on the next iteration of
behavioral training as a

36
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.120
load-bearing fix is a doomed bet.

37
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.640
To guarantee safety, the trust boundary
must be moved completely

38
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.080
outside the model.

39
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.720
The alternative is architectural safety.

40
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.960
This approach relies on deterministic
code, strict isolation boundaries, and

41
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.840
policy gates that entirely surround the
model.

42
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.280
In an architectural system, the model's
own refusal is strictly

43
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.720
a best effort layer.

44
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.000
The deployment architecture surrounding it
is authoritative.

45
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.800
To see how this works in practice, look at
embodied

46
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.760
AI, specifically vision-language action
models driving humanoid robotics.

47
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.960
Embodied AI operates in a kinetic action
space.

48
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.600
It can take physical actions that are
impossible to fully

49
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.760
anticipate or restrict using training data
alone.

50
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.400
The architectural solution here is a
cognitive cage, a deterministic

51
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.520
safety hypervisor that wraps the neural
network.

52
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.000
This hypervisor intercepts every single
motor command the model proposes,

53
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.800
verifying it against hard, deterministic
physical limits, like maximum force

54
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.800
and velocity. If the model hallucinates an
unsafe action, the

55
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.960
deterministic layer simply rejects the
command before the actuator ever

56
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.280
fires. Affordance hallucinations in
robotics do not result in kinetic

57
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.200
trauma when the action space is
constrained by unyielding code,

58
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.680
rather than the model's own sense of
caution.

59
00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.520
This exact same principle applies to
cognitive and emotional deployment

60
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.160
services, such as therapeutic AI.

61
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.080
Relying on a probabilistic model to
accurately self-identify when a

62
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.680
user's input escalates into a genuine
human crisis is a

63
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.000
high-stakes vulnerability. The
architectural fix involves three layers, starting with

64
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.480
pre-model input classification.

65
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.960
Deterministic classifiers analyze the
incoming message for crisis signals.

66
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.920
If triggered, the system routes the user
to a human

67
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.200
protocol and completely hides the prompt
from the model.

68
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.640
For normal inputs that do reach the model,
the architecture

69
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.960
applies a constrained output surface.

70
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.400
It prevents the model from generating free
text responses.

71
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.520
The model is forced to emit data into
structured schema

72
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.480
fields. This allows deterministic
validators to analyze, rewrite, or refuse

73
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.800
any outputs that violate policy.

74
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.840
The decision to escalate a crisis to a
human must

75
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.080
be externalized to the architecture.

76
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.000
The model is completely removed from
making high-stakes emotional judgment

77
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.720
calls. The challenge compounds in
multi-agent systems, where independent models

78
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.720
communicate directly with one another.

79
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.320
Single-agent safety alignments break down
here because agents constantly generate

80
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.920
inputs that neither model was explicitly
trained to handle.

81
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:49.240
The architectural solution is a semantic
firewall, a trust boundary

82
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.920
enforcement layer that sits at the
protocol level between agents.

83
00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:57.560
This firewall assumes every interagent
message is untrusted by default,

84
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.720
explicitly classifying and sanitizing
payloads before any reasoning is allowed

85
00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.880
to occur. The architecture explicitly
gates information sharing.

86
00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.040
No agent implicitly inherits the
potentially toxic context window of

87
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.400
another. When AI systems talk to each
other, you cannot

88
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.360
rely on making each individual agent
safer.

89
00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.120
True safety must live in the mediated,
heavily guarded protocol

90
00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.960
between them. Every safety architecture
shares five moves.

91
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.920
One, wrap the model in deterministic code.

92
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.800
Two, explicitly bound its action surface.

93
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.440
Three, assume adversarial inputs.

94
00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.000
Four, gate failure modes at runtime..

95
00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.440
5 The trust boundary lives in the
architecture, reducing the

96
00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.000
model to an overridable component.

97
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.600
Any system missing two or more pillars
relies on training

98
00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.120
time hope rather than a rigorous safety
architecture.

99
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.840
When you apply these pillars to the
current industry, the

100
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.440
gap becomes clear.

101
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.920
Most press releases announcing new AI
safety techniques describe behavioral

102
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.480
training, not safety architecture.

103
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.200
Deploying model cards, evaluation reports,
and capability tests without hard

104
00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.760
runtime limits is compliance theater.

105
00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.480
It means an organization has done the
model work, but

106
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.240
not the safety work.

107
00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.480
Giving any behaviorally aligned model
direct, unmediated access to an

108
00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.200
action space is categorically dangerous,
regardless of its benchmark scores.

109
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.760
This distinction matters for regulation.

110
00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.840
Mandating a safe model is legally
unenforceable because you cannot

111
00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.000
audit a probabilistic system into
compliance.

112
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.840
Policymakers must focus on regulating the
deployment architecture.

113
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.400
Mandating runtime layers with specific
properties moves AI policy from

114
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.840
mathematically impossible to practically
enforceable.

115
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.240
Moving from behavioral to architectural
safety shifts the heavy cost

116
00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.920
away from the training cluster and onto
the runtime deployment,

117
00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:53.400
requiring scarce engineering talent.

118
00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.400
While this runtime architecture is complex
and imperfect, the cost

119
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.840
is honest, it is transparent, and it is
verifiable in

120
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.200
the deployed code.

121
00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.440
Behavioral alignment will never
mathematically catch the infinite edge cases

122
00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.200
of reality. Safety must be built into the
deterministic system

123
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:13.800
surrounding the model, or it does not
exist anywhere at

124
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.440
all.

